    <PackageReleaseNotes>
10.10.0 - A rejected request now produces one log line naming the reason, instead of a
"Certificate authentication failed." line on nearly every call.

ACTION REQUIRED - authentication bypass risk. Search your codebase for AuthenticateResult.Failure
used against the certificate scheme. Code shaped like "if (result.Failure != null) return
Unauthorized();" no longer rejects a request that presented no client certificate, because that case
now returns NoResult() and Failure is null. Such code would admit an unauthenticated caller after
this upgrade. Branch on result.Succeeded instead, which is false for both cases and always was. The
library is fail-closed on every path reachable through the authorization pipeline - HttpContext.User
is never populated and a protected endpoint still returns 401 - but a host that inspects Failure
directly must be checked by hand.

No action needed to get the new logging if you register authorization through this library. Every
AddAuthorizationFor* extension now wires the rejection logger itself, so upgrading is enough and no call
site changes. Pass logAuthorizationRejections: false to opt out - but note that opting out leaves the
certificate handler and validator silent with nothing replacing them, so you would get less logging than
10.9.1, not more. Applications that build authorization WITHOUT those extensions must call
services.AddAuthorizationRejectionLogging() themselves.

Why this changed. CertificateAuthenticationHandler logged at Information for every failed
authentication. Because ASP.NET Core authenticates the default scheme on every request before
routing, and because a reverse proxy that terminates TLS forwards the ssl-client-* headers whether or
not a client certificate was used (no certificate arrives as ssl-client-verify: NONE), the handler
ran and failed on essentially all traffic - anonymous endpoints, health checks and swagger included.
A handler also only knows its own scheme, so it could never report an invalid token.

What replaces it. AuthorizationRejectionLogger implements IAuthorizationMiddlewareResultHandler, runs
once per request, and writes a single Information line when the result is a 401 challenge, naming the
reason: no credentials presented, an invalid certificate, an invalid or expired token. It reads each
scheme's already-cached AuthenticateResult, so it costs no extra authentication work, and it
delegates to the built-in handler so challenge and forbid behavior is unchanged. A request that is
not rejected - an anonymous endpoint, or one another scheme already authenticated - produces nothing.
403 (authenticated but lacking a role) is out of scope and produces no line.

Getting to strictly one line. The framework's own AuthenticationHandler diagnostics ("... was not
authenticated", "... was challenged") use the handler type as their logger category and cannot be
suppressed from this library. AuthorizationRejectionLogger therefore logs under its own category, so
raising the handler categories to Warning in your logging configuration leaves exactly one line per
401. See the XML docs on AddAuthorizationRejectionLogging for the category names. One more caveat: if you
use UseStatusCodePagesWithReExecute, mark the error endpoint [AllowAnonymous]. Otherwise the re-executed
request is itself rejected by the fallback policy and you get a second line, for the error path, on the
same client 401.

Weigh that filter before applying it. The certificate handler and validator no longer log, so the
framework's "was not authenticated" line is the only remaining signal for a bad credential presented on a
request that did NOT end in 401 - a forged or expired certificate probed against an [AllowAnonymous]
health, swagger or error endpoint, or one rescued by another scheme. Raising the category to Warning
discards that signal at the source. If you do security monitoring, prefer leaving the application at
Information and filtering at the log sink, where rules can match on message text: one line per 401 in the
dashboard, probe traffic still in storage.

Monitoring rules to re-check. 10.9.1 emitted the certificate header values as discrete structured fields
(SslclientSubjectDn, SslClientIssuerDn, SslClientVerify) at Information. They keep their exact names and
shapes but now log at Debug, so a rule relying on them needs the CertificateValidator category raised to
Debug. At Information the same values appear only as free text inside RejectionReason. The scheme name is
additionally exposed as a queryable RejectionScheme field, attached through a logging scope - so it
reaches only providers configured to capture scopes (IncludeScopes).

Other changes worth knowing. The rejection reason is truncated at 512 characters, since it can carry
caller-supplied header content. If your application sets IdentityModelEventSource.ShowPII = true as a
debugging aid, JWT failure messages stop redacting token and key material and that material would reach
this log line. If your application registers its own IAuthorizationMiddlewareResultHandler, this library
now replaces it by default and logs a Warning at startup naming the displaced type - pass
logAuthorizationRejections: false to keep yours. ExternalSystemNotFound now carries the certificate
subject DN in its message, so an unknown-subject rejection names the subject instead of only the
exception type. CertificateValidator's constructor is unchanged: it still takes a logger, and still logs
the three certificate validation failures - at Debug rather than Information. The missing-certificate case
(ssl-client-verify NONE or absent) logs nothing at any level, since that is every request.

Also in this release: AuthenticateResult.Fail(exception) replaces Fail(string), so a failure carries
the original exception rather than an AuthenticationFailureException wrapping a constant message. No
HTTP status code changes on any path reachable through the authorization pipeline, and nothing
authenticates that did not authenticate before. Two knock-on effects of that: the framework's own
"... was not authenticated. Failure message: ..." line now interpolates the concrete exception message
instead of a constant, so monitoring keyed on that line's text must be updated; and a host that surfaces
AuthenticateResult.Failure.Message in a response body now echoes that concrete message. The duplicate
certificate subject DN fault is
unchanged: still HTTP 500, still logged at Error by the handler itself, because it throws and never
reaches the authorization middleware.

All published packages must be upgraded together. See
docs/adr/records/2026-09-14-single-authorization-rejection-log-per-401.md.
    </PackageReleaseNotes>
